deep.reconstruction:
This page continues the bookkeeping of §§ around (134)–(147) in the second part of the 'Longue Marche' sequel, 'variations sur l'équation des lacets'. The objects appear to be algebraic group schemes built from a reductive group G (very plausibly GL2-like). Its derived/special group is SG and its adjoint group is PG. T_ρ and N'_σ are a torus and a torus-normaliser-type group attached to two automorphisms ρ, σ. The indices 3 and 2 on the characters ε_3, ε_2 suggest elements of order 3 and 2, i.e. the PSL2(Z)-type generators that enter the 'loop equation'. On the previous page Grothendieck introduced a group M_{ρ,σ} together with:
- characters χ: M→G_m and (ε_3,ε_2): M→μ2×μ2;
- subgroups M^+ = Ker χ, M^∘ = Ker(ε_3,ε_2) and M^{+∘} = M^+ ∩ M^∘;
- R_{ρ,σ} = Ker(M→PG), SR_{ρ,σ} = Ker(M→G), and their ∘-variants;
- product decompositions (147) of these groups.

(148) now takes as the definition of M_{ρ,σ} the central product M = (R_{ρ,σ} × SG)/μ2. Here μ2 is embedded antidiagonally, as a central subgroup common to R (via its G_m factor) and to SG (as ±1). The same construction with R^∘ gives M^∘. The vertical maps R^∘×SG → R×SG and M^∘ → M are inclusions with cokernel μ2×μ2, and the identity on μ2 is compatible with them.

(149) asserts that on the χ-kernel the extension splits: M^+ ≃ SR × SG and M^{+∘} ≃ SR^∘ × SG. This is consistent because SR has trivial G_m factor (147), so μ2 meets SR×1 trivially.

(150) defines T_{ρ,σ} = M/SG, with SG embedded 'diag' (as the second factor of the central product). Its subgroups T^∘, T^+ and T^{+∘} are quotients of M^∘, M^+ and M^{+∘} by SG. They are described as kernels of the induced maps T→μ2×μ2, T→G_m and T→G_m×μ2×μ2, with T^{+∘} = T^∘ ∩ T^+.

The page then records:
- the extensions 1→SG→M→T→1, and the analogous ones for the ∘, + and +∘ variants;
- the exact sequence 1→T^∘→T→μ2×μ2→1, and similar sequences for the inclusions T^+⊂T, T^{+∘}⊂T, T^{+∘}⊂T^+ and T^{+∘}⊂T^∘.

The next page completes this with T ≃ R/μ2, T^∘ ≃ R^∘/μ2, T^+ ≃ SR and T^{+∘} ≃ SR^∘. Purpose: to set up a clean dictionary of group schemes (and their Γ-points) in which the unknowns of the loop equation (u, α, β, f, a, b of the next page) live. These are the 'prismatic' diagrams of commensurable subgroups referred to earlier.
